description:
MmsValue_decodeMmsData parses a BER/ASN.1-encoded "MMS Data" element (as used in IEC 61850/MMS read responses, journal entries and write-service requests) from an untrusted byte buffer into a heap-allocated MmsValue tree. It is directly security-relevant: the decoder recursively walks attacker-controlled tag/length/value triplets to build nested arrays and structures, allocates buffers sized from attacker-controlled length fields (bit strings, octet strings, integers, visible/MMS strings, binary time, UTC time), and must defend against malformed/oversized/negative lengths and unbounded recursion. A natural companion feature is the inverse operation, MmsValue_encodeMmsData, which re-serializes the decoded tree, and MmsValue_printToBuffer, which walks/dumps the resulting tree — both are realistic "consumers" that exercise the decoded structure further and are exactly what the existing in-tree fuzz harnesses (fuzz_mms_decode/print/encode) and the server read-response/journal/write-service code paths do with the result.
- MmsValue_decodeMmsData: target; top-level entry that calls MmsValue_decodeMmsDataMaxRecursion with a fixed depth of 25; parses one BER tag/length/value element and dispatches on the tag byte.
- MmsValue_decodeMmsDataMaxRecursion: variant/config; same decoder but lets the caller select the maximum recursion depth, directly controlling how deep nested array/structure tags (0xa1/0xa2) can be before MmsValue_decodeMmsDataRecursive aborts.
- MmsValue_encodeMmsData: consumer/inverse; re-encodes the MmsValue tree produced by the decoder back into BER bytes (supports a length-only pass and an actual-encode pass), exercising encodeArrayAccessResult/encodeStructuredAccessResult and all the per-type encoders — a natural round-trip check on the decoded object graph.
- MmsValue_printToBuffer: consumer; serializes the decoded MmsValue tree (including nested arrays/structures) to a human-readable string, walking the whole structure and thus validating consistency of every field the decoder filled in (bitstring padding, integer/float sizes, string contents, time buffers).

Input space (selected by the fuzzed buffer bytes and by the harness choosing which entries/params to call):
- BER tag byte dispatch in MmsValue_decodeMmsDataRecursive/getNumberOfElements: 0xa1 (array), 0xa2 (structure, recursive), 0x80 (data-access-error), 0x83 (boolean), 0x84 (bit string, with a padding-bits byte 0-7 that must be validated), 0x85 (integer, length must be ≤8), 0x86 (unsigned, length ≤8), 0x87 (float, length must be exactly 5 or 9 for float/double), 0x89 (octet string), 0x8a (visible string), 0x8c (binary time, length 4 or 6), 0x90 (MMS string), 0x91 (UTC time, length must be exactly 8), 0x00 (indefinite-length end tag), and any other tag value which must be rejected as an error;
- BER length encoding (short/long form, possibly malformed or pointing past bufferLength) decoded by BerDecoder_decodeLength, which determines dataLength and triggers the zero-length rejection rule (data length must be >0 unless tag is visible-string/mms-string/octet-string);
- nesting depth of array/structure elements vs. maxDepth, exercised via MmsValue_decodeMmsDataMaxRecursion's maxDepth parameter as well as the fixed 25-depth default used by MmsValue_decodeMmsData;
- number and length of sibling elements inside an array/structure as computed by getNumberOfElements, which itself re-walks the same tag space;
- bufPos/bufferLength/endBufPos offsets supplied directly by the harness, letting the fuzzer choose where decoding starts/stops within the input buffer;
- for the encode/print consumers: the `encode` boolean of MmsValue_encodeMmsData (length-only vs. real encode pass) and the printBuffer size for MmsValue_printToBuffer, both of which traverse whatever array/structure/type the decoder produced.
